# L §5.10 point 1, §5.19, E §4.2 steps 0 and 5 (tally 2.1.2, issue #46): an opening always anchors (L §5.19), so
# its place is tested in the day's first batch like an anchor's: y opens at -5.00 PLN, a claim (min 0), and nothing
# moves it on 2026-01-01; it is refused from the batch's end, `<place> is <b> after its reading at <instant>`. The
# start of the day is no point of the rule (L §5.10's three points), so bal(y, 2026-01-01 start) is the opening as
# stated. z opens at 0 and stands. tally 2.1.1's refcore: q (-5.00, -5.00, 0.00, 0.00) PLN.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-06-30
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01
place y: claims PLN lives 2026-01-01..
place z: claims PLN lives 2026-01-01..
place world: outside lives 2026-01-01..
input amt: money PLN
input day: date
input day2: date

move top: transfers
  on day2
  leg cash from world to z amount amt

move p: transfers
  on day
  reads y, z
  leg cash from world to y amount amt + 0 * bal(y) + 0 * bal(z)

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  amt = 10.00 PLN
  day = 2026-02-10
  day2 = 2026-01-15
  opening[y] = -5.00 PLN at 2026-01-01 start
  opening[z] = 0 PLN at 2026-01-01 start

ask q: bal(y, 2026-01-01 start), bal(y, 2026-01-01), bal(z, 2026-01-01), bal(world, PLN, 2026-01-01)
=== expect
q: (-5.00 PLN, refused: RUN-PLACE y is -5.00 PLN after its reading at 2026-01-01 start, 0.00 PLN, 0.00 PLN)
  cut 2026-06-30
